sub:assertion {
<https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/thompson1917-ch07-12> a <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/Claim>;
    dc:description "The regular hexagonal and pyramidal geometry of the bee’s comb is produced automatically by surface tension acting on the cell walls while the wax (or the wasp’s paper pulp) is still semi-fluid, the insects merely building thin tubular cells packed closely together; it is not due to the bee seeking to economise wax.";
    dc:type "mechanism";
    <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Comb geometry arises from surface tension in semi-fluid wax, not bee design";
    <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/context> "D’Arcy Wentworth Thompson, On Growth and Form (1st ed., Cambridge: Cambridge University Press, 1917), Chapter VII, “The Forms of Tissues, or Cell-Aggregates”; topic “The bee’s cell”. Scope: comb-building social bees and wasps"
}
